# Until Looking Glass replaces Electron (docs/eravos-v2-emerge-looking-glass.md) — then the GPU-first renderer is the answer.

## What is true today (read from the code, 2026-11-24)
  - desktop/main.js sets no GPU / V8 / Chromium switches → Electron's defaults: hardware acceleration on (the GPU composites the page,
    CSS transforms and opacity, 2D canvas where Chromium accelerates it). One tool draws with WebGL (particle-field); every other display
    (spectra, meters, wires, scopes, the piano roll) is 2D canvas or DOM on the main thread.
  - Threads: all sound = one real-time audio thread (Web Audio; AudioWorklets run there); all UI JavaScript = one main thread; plus the GPU
    process, the compositor, the precision-clock Worker; image-core, dataflow and code-cell have workers of their own. ≈ 3–4 cores busy.
    No OffscreenCanvas, no SharedArrayBuffer, no WebAssembly DSP.
  - 15 files run their own requestAnimationFrame loop (canvas-wm, wire-system, colour-field, glass-layer, ui-kit, sound-css, react-panel,
    the mixer, timeline + piano roll, song-preview, songbook, the music domain, the Field, the video clip, the precision clock).
  - 3.147 measured the main thread 5.9 of 6 s busy with one tool sounding (reactive colours + glow) → they keep a time budget since.
  - RAM: one renderer is capped near 4 GB by V8; ERAVOS is far below it — not the bottleneck.
  - His machine: Ryzen 7 3700X (8 cores / 16 threads) · GTX 1650 (4 GB) · 16 GB RAM.

## Decisions
  - D1 Live sound never leaves the CPU's audio thread (no GPU audio: the round trip costs latency and glitches). It is made faster, not split.
  - D2 The GPU draws pictures (displays, wires, the 3D note view) and may compute OFFLINE work (analysis, measuring a made tool).
  - D3 Animations move only transform and opacity (compositor-only); reduced motion and Performance mode turn them off.
  - D4 PRIORITY FOLLOWS THE CANVAS, AGNOSTIC (his rule): the canvas never names a domain; each tool's contract declares the resources it
    uses; the canvas sums the needs of what is on it and gives priority from that. Music only → the audio thread first; images only → the
    GPU and image memory first, no audio engine started; both → both, by how many ask. A tool removed takes its claim with it.
  - D5 Every phase is measured before and after on his machine (PF0); the sound never changes (null render) at any step.

## Resource needs (RP — the contract part; agnostic)
  - RP1 tool.json `needs.resources`: [ 'audio-realtime' | 'draw' | 'draw-gpu' | 'image-memory' | 'video-decode' | 'cpu-background' |
    'midi-in' | 'files' ], written from each tool's DAG (build/tool-needs.mjs, beside its components and UI elements). A check: every
    tool declares them (CI).
  - RP2 the canvas's ResourcePlan: the sum over the tools on the canvas → which engines start, thread priorities, frame budgets per kind of
    drawing, what Performance mode turns down first. Domain-free (tests/boundary.test.js).
  - RP3 the same needs drive Settings' sections (specs/eravos.settings-buttons-dag.map.spec ADDENDUM 2026-11-24, ST3).

## Phases (bottom up; each measured)
  - PF0 MEASURE: Settings › Performance shows GPU status (app.getGPUFeatureStatus), frame times, main-thread busy %, audio-thread load,
    worker count, memory; a one-click report saved to the library. Gate for every later phase.
  - PF1 PAUSE WHAT IS NOT SEEN + LAZY: one shared frame scheduler (the 15 loops become clients with budgets); a window off screen, behind
    another, minimised or folded draws nothing; a tool's full face built on first sight.
  - PF2 PERFORMANCE MODE: a switch (Settings › Performance) — no transitions, no reactive colours / glow, meters at a lower rate; follows
    Windows' reduce-motion. Off by default (today's look stays the default).
  - PF3 ZOOMED OUT = SIMPLER ("real pixelated when zooming out"): below a zoom level a tool is one cached picture (or its name, colour and
    a level); its live controls come back as it is zoomed into. Sound untouched.
  - PF4 TOOLS THAT GROW AS THEY ARE RESIZED ("a simplified ui for tools. then they expand as you resize them"): small = the essentials
    (face, the key knobs, keys); wider = more, up to today's full window. Built from each tool's element list (the element synthesis, ES1,
    3.183). A LOOK CHANGE → a preview he approves first; today's full face stays the large size.
  - PF5 DISPLAYS ON THE GPU: spectra, scopes, meters, waveforms, wires → WebGL (one shared context), or OffscreenCanvas in workers.
  - PF6 A WORKER POOL ACROSS THE CORES: offline work — synthesizing and judging a made tool (assembled-tool.js render + novelty), mutation
    measuring, bouncing, analysis — in workers (navigator.hardwareConcurrency − 2). His 16 threads used.
  - PF7 FASTER SOUND PER CORE: the shared DSP kernels (filters, oscillators, …) in WebAssembly + SIMD inside the worklets; proven
    bit-close by tests/components.mjs and the null render.
  - PF8 SAFE CHROMIUM SWITCHES: GPU rasterization / zero-copy where supported; never ignore-gpu-blocklist (driver crashes).

## Animations (AN — "i want beautiful animations in eravos")
  - AN1 one motion language everywhere, from the shared FieldMotion (tools/core/field/motion.js): windows open and close, tools land on
    the canvas, wires draw in, menus unfold, Start's two steps flow into each other (StartScreen's cascade, specs/eravos.start-screens.spec).
  - AN2 transform + opacity only (D3); reduced motion and Performance mode → at once.
  - AN3 SHOWN AS A VIDEO FIRST (the rule: show him first), then built.

## Order
  PF0 → PF1 → PF2 → PF3 → (PF4 preview → his yes → PF4) → PF5 → PF6 → PF7; RP1 → RP2 → RP3 alongside PF1; AN1–AN3 after PF1 (a smooth
  canvas first, then motion on it). Tracked in docs/TODO-LEDGER.md (T-13 … T-24).
